description.narrative:
A key pillar of the program is strengthening financial resilience through resource mobilization training, direct organization development initiatives and linking the partners to other potential funders.&nbsp;The Pathway to Sustainability (PTS) workshops were implemented in 4 countries in South East Asia, West Africa and East Africa. These focused on donor engagement, proposal writing and strategic planning. These workshops encouraged collaboration, fostered donor relationships, and inspired calls for more regular training. The PTS workshops were well received, with participants emphasizing the need for regular training to sustain their organizations. The workshops also fostered cross-program learning and networking, enabling organizations to build stronger relationships with donors and partners. Facilitators in these regions demonstrated exceptional teamwork and dedication, ensuring the successful rollout of the PTS ​​​​program. One of the significant outcomes include critical funding wins. 26 partners in West Africa, South East Asia, MENA and Southern Africa received organization capacity strengtheninggrants from Hivos. These grants enabled the organizations to enhance their organizational structures, develop organization policies and improved their sustainability. Several organizations benefited from targeted capacity-building initiatives, including support in refining internal policies, improving financial management systems, and enhancing strategic planning. These efforts contributed not only to improved operational efficiency but also to increased donor confidence.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Across the F2BM focus countries, strategic advocacy, coalition-building, and legal empowerment have played a critical role in advancing protections and policy shifts for LGBTIQ+ communities. Despite political constraints and growing restrictive policy environments, especially in the African continent, the Coalition of Actions (CoA) have found innovative ways to push for legal protection, infiltrating through policymakers, and building strong coalitions that amplify their collective voices. The CoA's legal and Policy change technical working groups share a commitment to fostering human rights, supporting legal reform, and building advocacy platforms that challenge societal exclusion. In South East Asia and West Africa, CoAs received training that helped enhance their lobbying and advocacy skills, equipping them to advocate for LGBTIQ+ rights at global forums such as the Commission on the Status of Women (CSW) and the High-Level Political Forum on Sustainable Development (HLPF). The MENA region too, benefitted from one COA’s efforts to organize events and facilitate discussions with government officials, contributing to the larger discourse on human rights in the region. Through these initiatives, the program has empowered LGBTIQ+ organizations to engage more effectively with international bodies, ensuring that local struggles are represented on a global scale.&nbsp;Meanwhile, at the African Commission on Human and Peoples’ Rights, advocacy efforts led to a significant breakthrough. The Commission’s Chairperson publicly condemned the rise in homophobia and violent attacks against LGBTIQ+ persons across the continent.
Beyond national advocacy, one alliance partner played a critical role in strengthening legal protections through global human rights mechanisms. Over the year, their work with Universal Periodic Review (UPR) and treaty body mechanisms influenced legal recommendations in countries including in Africa and South East Asia. The emphasis was not only on decriminalization but also on ensuring access to healthcare, education, and employment for LGBTIQ+ persons. Collaborating with CoAs in West Africa, Southern Africa and South East Asia, one alliance member worked to develop national implementation strategies for legal and policy changes, reinforcing the importance of systematic, long-term advocacy.
A key milestone this year was the recalibration and evolution of the economic empowerment pathway. The implementation of the revised design which focuses on influencing policies and programs for income support, job creation, micro, small and medium enterprise development, skills training was rolled out in August 2024 in 11 F2BM countries except one country in the MENA region where the political and conflict situation led them to focus on building strong foundations. However, implementation in one country in East Africa was halted due to the unfortunate theft incident. Following technical capacity-strengthening efforts, a series of lobbying efforts, workshops, and public discussions were conducted with the goals of creating safe spaces and connecting SOGIESC issues with the business sector, international and regional financial institutions, and labor unions. These were done at national, regional and global level.&nbsp;
&nbsp;LILO UP! was piloted in Africa, in February 2024 involving 12 participants focusing on economic empowerment, self-awareness, and practical skills for employment and entrepreneurship. Participants reported personal growth, improved financial management, and a renewed sense of economic justice. Follow-up "Stepping stones" sessions at third, ninth, and twelve-months reinforced progress, with initial feedback showing significant improvements in financial independence, savings, and future planning. A Global Training of Trainers held in November 2024, with facilitators from 10 countries, incorporated monitoring, evaluation, and learning components to document outcomes and support local advocacy plans. The program aims to connect participants to broader economic opportunities and will expand rollouts in 3 other countries in East and West Africa.&nbsp;The training addressed financial disparities within the LGBTIQ+ community through initiatives such as the LILO UP Methodology Workshop, which engaged participants in financial literacy, career planning, and entrepreneurship. Despite varying levels of financial literacy among attendees, the workshop enabled participants to create personalized budgets, develop business plans, and establish peer mentorship networks. One participant expanded their business by learning massage therapy, boosting their income. Their growing success allowed them to invest in more land and livestock. Now, they aim to mentor young queer individuals in agri-poultry, fostering skill-sharing and community support. Another participant initially set their "Golden Goal" as launching an M&amp;E consultancy but adapted when funding challenges arose. They pivoted to studying abroad, now focusing on digital technology for social impact, a field they’re passionate about. The Golden Goal exercise helped them reassess priorities while staying aligned with their aspirations.
&nbsp;While everyone deserves equal opportunities and a safe and inclusive work environment, F2BM studies and engagements in 2024 showed there is still consistent, persistent and widespread discrimination against LGBTIQ+ individuals in the workplace. One alliance member conducted an online training session using their Civil Society Organization (CSO) toolkit on LGBTIQ+ civil society advocacy with corporate companies. The training dug deeper into advocacy with businesses and was done with Africa and South East Asia. These resulted in the research on Diversity Equity and Inclusion (DEI) landscape in West Africa. The reports should be ready later on in 2025.&nbsp;
&nbsp;In South East Asia, a study on LGBTIQ+ socio-economic conditions highlighted discrimination as a key barrier to economic opportunities, especially for intersex individuals. This research informed local advocacy efforts and led to economic empowerment workshops in eight localities. The CoA developed a framework to integrate socio-economic development into projects, analyzed the World Bank Group Gender Strategy 2024-2030, and trained 29 organizations in lobbying, with plans for a 2025 campaign targeting international financial institutions including World Bank and Asia Development Bank. The lobbying and campaign plan, developed after training, will complement advocacy targeting multilateral organizations, embassies, and other relevant financial institutions. Another research conducted by Edelman on LGBTIQ+ inclusivity in multinational and national companies highlighted workplace barriers, leading to policy recommendations aimed at fostering more inclusive hiring practices. This will serve as an advocacy tool to engage businesses in 2025 and beyond.
